<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="seq-families">
  <title>Special sequences</title>
  <introduction><p>Some families have especially useful rules. Learn to <em>recognise</em> the structure before memorising a formula.</p></introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="seq-family-arithmetic">
    <title>Arithmetic sequences: add the same amount</title>
    <p>An <term>arithmetic sequence</term> has a constant <term>common difference</term> <m>d</m> between consecutive terms. For example, <m>1,4,7,10,13,\ldots</m> has <m>d=3</m>.</p>
    <me>T_n=a+(n-1)d,\qquad a=T_1.</me>
    <p>The <m>n-1</m> counts how many jumps occur between the first and the <m>n</m>th term. Differences may be positive, zero or negative.</p>
    <p>For your visual method, continue to <xref ref="sequences-intro"/> to reveal the signed differences beneath the terms and work backwards to the boxed term zero.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="seq-arith-ex"><statement><p>Find the common difference and tenth term of <m>14,10,6,2,\ldots</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>d=-4</m>, so <m>T_{10}=14+9(-4)=-22</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="seq-family-geometric">
    <title>Geometric sequences: multiply by the same factor</title>
    <p>A <term>geometric sequence</term> has a constant <term>common ratio</term> <m>r</m>. For example, <m>2,6,18,54,\ldots</m> multiplies by <m>3</m>.</p>
    <me>T_n=ar^{n-1},\qquad a=T_1.</me>
    <p>Here <m>T_{10}=2(3)^9</m>. A ratio between <m>0</m> and <m>1</m> describes decay; a negative ratio alternates the signs. A ratio of <m>0</m> produces zero after the first term.</p>
    <p>Go to <xref ref="sequences-geometric"/> for diagrams, depreciation, and geometric sums.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="seq-geo-ex"><statement><p>Write the next two terms of <m>64,32,16,8,\ldots</m>. State <m>r</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>4,2</m>; the common ratio is <m>r=\frac12</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="seq-family-triangular">
    <title>Triangular numbers: add another row</title>
    <p>Arrange dots in rows of lengths <m>1,2,3,\ldots</m>. The first triangle has one dot; the next has <m>1+2=3</m>; then <m>1+2+3=6</m>. The totals form</p>
    <me>1,\ 3,\ 6,\ 10,\ 15,\ 21,\ldots</me>
    <p>The <m>n</m>th triangular number is</p>
    <me>T_n=1+2+\cdots+n=\frac{n(n+1)}2.</me>
    <table xml:id="seq-triangular-growing-table">
      <title>Each new row grows the triangle</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes"><cell>Rows</cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell><cell><m>5</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Dots added</cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell><cell><m>5</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Total dots</cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>6</m></cell><cell><m>10</m></cell><cell><m>15</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <exercise xml:id="seq-triangle-ex"><statement><p>How many dots are in a triangular pattern with 20 rows? What is the difference between the 20th and the 19th triangular numbers?</p></statement><answer><p><m>T_{20}=20(21)/2=210</m>. The newest row adds <m>20</m> dots, so the difference is 20.</p></answer></exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="seq-family-squares-cubes">
    <title>Square numbers and cube numbers</title>
    <p>The square numbers count dots arranged as <m>n</m> rows of <m>n</m> dots. The cube numbers count unit cubes in an <m>n</m> by <m>n</m> by <m>n</m> block.</p>
    <me>\underbrace{1,4,9,16,25,\ldots}_{T_n=n^2}
    \qquad
    \underbrace{1,8,27,64,125,\ldots}_{T_n=n^3}</me>
    <p>Square numbers have constant <em>second differences</em>. Explore that connection in <xref ref="sequences-quadratic"/>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="seq-sq-cube-ex"><statement><p>Is <m>64</m> a square number, a cube number, or both? Give its position in each sequence.</p></statement><answer><p>Both: <m>8^2=64</m> (the eighth square) and <m>4^3=64</m> (the fourth cube).</p></answer></exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="seq-family-fibonacci">
    <title>Fibonacci numbers: use two earlier terms</title>
    <p>Start at <m>F_0=0</m> and <m>F_1=1</m>. Add the previous two terms to obtain each new term:</p>
    <me>0,\ 1,\ 1,\ 2,\ 3,\ 5,\ 8,\ 13,\ 21,\ldots</me>
    <me>F_n=F_{n-1}+F_{n-2}\qquad(n\geq2).</me>
    <p>Unlike an arithmetic or geometric sequence, this recursive rule needs <em>two</em> starting values. Notice that the first term is labelled <m>F_0</m>, not <m>F_1</m>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="seq-fib-ex"><statement><p>Find <m>F_7</m>, <m>F_8</m> and <m>F_9</m>, starting from <m>F_5=5</m> and <m>F_6=8</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>F_7=13</m>, <m>F_8=21</m> and <m>F_9=34</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
